\title{Pointer arithmetic flags that are not exposed yet}
\taxon{Problem}
\date{2026-11-13}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{schrodingerzy}
\tag{language}
\tag{compiler}
\meta{status}{open}
\meta{created}{2026-11-13}
\import{base-macros}

\p{\strong{Status:} open.}
\ul{
\li{\code{\verb~|advance<T>(p, count, flag)~} multiplies \code{\verb~|count~} by the size of \code{\verb~|T~} with a wrapping multiplication and puts \code{\verb~|flag~} on the \code{\verb~|ptr.ptr_add~} only. This is sound and weaker than a typed \code{\verb~|getelementptr~} with the same flag, whose scaling is covered by the flag. Carrying \code{\verb~|nsw~} or \code{\verb~|nuw~} on the multiplication needs an overflow-flag attribute that the code generator does not build today.}
\li{\code{\verb~|diff~} always emits a \code{\verb~|ptr.ptr_diff~} without \code{\verb~|nuw~} or \code{\verb~|nsw~}, and there is no exact element distance.}
\li{\code{\verb~|load_extended~} and \code{\verb~|store_extended~} have no atomic ordering, although the conversion patterns handle one.}
}
\p{\strong{What would close this.} A decision on the surface for each, then the builders. Source: the review recorded in [ses-5R5K](ses-5R5K).}
